at a small fishing village just south of  Santiago Chile  Jose Barrios and other fishermen are  getting ready to head out to sea  things here appear calm but a state of  emergency remains in effect in the  country and it's a subject that's on  everyone's mind what's happening in the  country isn't about politics this is a  social problem  Chile's president has announced reforms  to social programs in an effort to calm  the crisis but Jose says people have  lost confidence in political leaders the  president of the Republic has only  patched a wound but there is still a  wound underneath until that wound is  treated and heals the problems of our  country are not going away  China is Latin America's most developed  nation but over the past week protests  have highlighted a growing angst among  the citizenry the unrest began over an  increase of 30 pesos - the cost of metro  transport which amounts to about 10  cents of a dollar that cost increase was  like a spark to a powder keg in a  country where many were already  complaining about a worsening wealth gap  between the rich and the middle class  Chile economists like Patricio Navya say  a solution out of the crisis will  require a delicate compromise from the  country's political elite and the  perception is that Chile is not in a  crisis she is in a difficult situation  but we are developed enough for everyone  to have better living conditions so it's  a matter of distribution if we can  better distribute the wealth and the  opportunities that exist in Chile  everyone is going to be better off  after a week of unrest demonstrations in  China have spread to other parts of the  country a recent national poll found  that 83% of Chileans are in favor of the  ongoing social mobilizations even in  Sheila's smallest communities far away  from the protests that have paralyzed  the country's capital the discontent  from citizens is palpable for everyday  Chileans the crisis isn't about an  increase of 30 pesos to the cost o